use ../../lib/CodingRule

from ../CppCoreGuidelines import CppCoreGuidelines

export coding-rule CppCoreC4:
    standard: @{CppCoreGuidelines}
    ruleId: C.4
    title: Make a function a member only if it needs the representation
    statement:
        If a function can be written using only the public interface of a
        class, make it a non-member. Reserve member functions for operations
        that need direct access to private state.
    rationale:
        Fewer members means less coupling, fewer functions that can break
        the invariant, and fewer functions to revisit when the
        representation changes.
    exceptions:
        Virtual functions and the operators =, (), [] and -> must be
        members. Some members of an overload set or of a chainable set of
        functions may be members for consistency even if they do not touch
        private data.
    severity: null
    noncompliant:
        ```cpp
        \\\
        class Date {
        public:
            int year() const;
            int month() const;
            int day() const;
            Date next_weekday() const;   // uses only the accessors above
        private:
            int y_, m_, d_;
        };
        \\\
        ```
    compliant:
        ```cpp
        \\\
        class Date {
        public:
            int year() const;
            int month() const;
            int day() const;
        private:
            int y_, m_, d_;
        };

        Date next_weekday(const Date& d);
        \\\
        ```
    languages: [cpp]
    tags: [classes, coupling, encapsulation]
    source: https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#rc-member
